    <channel>
        <title>DuoUpdater</title>
        <!--
          The Sparkle feed. `scripts/publish-release.sh` regenerates this file on
          every release and pushes it here. The app reads it through
          https://updates.duoupdater.app/appcast.xml (workers/appcast relays this
          file); builds before that read
          https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jizhi0v0/duo-updater/main/appcast.xml
          directly, so this path must keep working.

          Deliberately empty rather than absent: an appcast with no items means
          "nothing newer available", which Sparkle handles as up-to-date, whereas
          a missing file is a fetch error on every check. The first release fills
          it in.

          Release history starts here. Earlier builds were published from a
          separate binary-only repo that existed only while the source was
          private, and are not carried over.
        -->
        <item>
            <title>0.5.14</title>
            <pubDate>Sat, 24 Oct 2026 21:00:21 +0800</pubDate>
            <link>https://github.com/jizhi0v0/duo-updater/releases/tag/v0.5.14</link>
            <sparkle:version>132</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.5.14</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>15.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description sparkle:format="markdown"><![CDATA[**mise tools are supported.** Each tool in mise's global config gets an update check and a one-click update within the version you asked for; a pinned tool shows what lies beyond the pin, with the command to move to it. They sit under Homebrew in the workbench's CLI tab.

**Other tools opens its own page in the menu bar.** The arrow beside Other tools lists each group, such as mise tools, Claude Code or npm under nvm, with its own Update and Cancel. Right-click a group to show it in the window.

**Homebrew casks without an app are listed.** A JDK such as zulu, or a cask whose app you deleted, now shows under Homebrew with its update, where before it appeared nowhere. Casks that install with a package ask for your password in DuoUpdater's own panel.

**DuoUpdater tells you when a developer has announced an app's end.** Arc (stops working on 2026-12-22) and ChatGPT Atlas (stopped on 2026-08-09) get a mark in the workbench and a notice linking to the announcement; `duo check` and `duo list` print it too.

**Gemini updates install during Google's staged rollouts.** They used to be refused at the last step when Google's second answer named the older version.

**The "could not be checked" banner names the apps.** Click it to show them in the list.
]]></description>
